        <h3>Section 1: Topic</h3>
        <div class="question">
          <p><strong>1.</strong> What is the output of the following code?

```python
class Example:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value
    def __str__(self):
        return f'Value is {self.value}'

obj = Example(5)
print(obj)
```
 <span class="marks">(25 marks)</span></p><div class="options">
            <div class="option">
              <span>A. None</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>B. Value is 5</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>C. <__main__.Example object at memory_location></span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>D. An error is raised.</span>
            </div></div></div>
        <div class="question">
          <p><strong>2.</strong> How do you define a constructor in a Python class? <span class="marks">(25 marks)</span></p><div class="options">
            <div class="option">
              <span>A. def __init__(self):</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>B. def constructor(self):</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>C. def construct(self):</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>D. def _construct(self):</span>
            </div></div></div>
        <div class="question">
          <p><strong>3.</strong> What is the primary purpose of using decorators in Python? <span class="marks">(25 marks)</span></p><div class="options">
            <div class="option">
              <span>A. To define class attributes.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>B. To implement recursion in functions.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>C. To modify the functionality of a function or class method.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>D. To decorate the syntax of functions.</span>
            </div></div></div>
        <div class="question">
          <p><strong>4.</strong> Which of the following statements is true regarding inheritance in Python? <span class="marks">(25 marks)</span></p><div class="options">
            <div class="option">
              <span>A. All Python classes must inherit from `object` explicitly.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>B. Python only supports single inheritance.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>C. Python supports multiple inheritance.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="option">
              <span>D. Inheritance cannot be used to override methods.</span>
            </div></div></div></div>
